ꜣz:
{{egy-rom|ꜣz}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-pr}}

===Noun===
{{egy-noun|m|head=A-z-G38}}

# {{def-unc|egy}} {{defdate|Coffin Texts and Middle Kingdom literature}}
## [[carrion]]; {{ng|perhaps an}} {{alternative form of|egy|ꜣjs||viscera, offal}}
## [[bald-headed]] [[vulture]]
## [[bird]] [[dropping]]s

====Usage notes====
If the sense ‘carrion’ or the like is to be preferred, this word is likely an orthographic variant of {{m|egy|ꜣjs||viscera, offal}}.{{ref|Allen, James P. (2011) ''The Debate Between a Man and His Soul: A Masterpiece of Ancient Egyptian Literature'', page 80:<br>
“The noun {{m|egy||ꜣsw}} is unattested elsewhere with this determinative. Most translations have followed either Scharf’s suggestion that it refers to bird droppings (1937, 44) or Blackman’s interpretation of it as a term for bald-headed vultures (1938, 67–68). Goedicke, who adopted Scharff’s surmise, proposed a connection with {{m|egy||ꜣs}} (1970, 146–477), more fully {{m|egy|ꜣjs}}, a general term for offal (''Wb.'' I, 20, 10–13; ''Wb. Drogennamen'', 1). The determinative here, in place of the usual “pustule,” suggests an image of carrion, as understood by a few scholars (Lichtheim 1973, 166; Lalouette 1984, 223; Assmann 1998, 394; Haller 2004, 17; Quirke 2004, 132).”}}{{ref|Iversen, Erik (1947) “Some remarks on the terms {{egy-h|a-m-m-H8}} and {{egy-h|A-i-s-Aa2}}”, in ''The Journal of Egyptian Archaeology'', volume 33, pages 47–51|name=Iversen}}

The rendering ‘bald-headed vulture’ is based on the verb {{m|egy|jꜣs||to be bald}} and originally due to Blackman, remarking on a passage in the ''{{w|Dispute Between a Man and His Ba}}'' which compares a stench to that of {{m|egy||ꜣz}};{{ref|Blackman, A. M. (1938) “[https://www.jstor.org/stable/43581192 A note on ‘Lebensmüder’, 86-88]”, in ''Orientalia'', volume 7, pages 67–68}} however, Blackman subsequently favored ‘viscera’ in this passage instead.{{ref|name=Iversen}} The word is also attested in the Coffin Texts, where in Spells 467 and 468 Osiris is called {{m|egy|kꜣ-ꜣz|lit=Bull of Carrion{{sup|?}}/Vultures{{sup|?}}}}; compare his parallel title {{m|egy|nb-ḥwꜣ|lit=Lord of Rot}} from the same passages.{{ref|{{R:egy:ECT|5|374 b and 385 o}}}}{{ref|{{R:egy:FECT|2|96 and 99}}}}

