wꜣj:
{{egy-rom|wꜣi̯}}

===Pronunciation===
{{egy-pr|wꜣ_j}}

===Etymology 1===
Perhaps compare {{cog|ar|وَرَاءَ||behind, beyond}}.{{ref|{{w|Joachim Friedrich Quack|Quack, Joachim Friedrich}} (1993), “[https://wwwuser.gwdguser.de/~lingaeg/pdfs/LingAeg_03-p59-79.pdf Ein altägyptisches Sprachtabu]” in ''Lingua Aegyptia'', volume 3, pages 59–79|name=Quack}}

====Verb====
{{egy-verb|3ae-inf|head=wA-A-N31}} {{tlb|egy|intransitive}}

# to be(come) [[distant]], [[remote]], [[far away]] (+ {{m|egy|r}}: from; to [[remove]] oneself from)
#: {{ant|egy|tkn}}
# {{lb|egy|with {{m|egy|r}}|of evil or evil things}} to [[stay away]] from (someone), to not come near or [[encounter]] (someone)
# {{lb|egy|with {{m|egy|r}} and {{m|egy|n}}}} to [[waive]] or [[remit]] ({{m|egy|n}}: something) from ({{m|egy|r}}: someone) {{defdate|Greco-Roman Period}}
# {{senseid|egy|fall into}} {{lb|egy|with {{m|egy|r}} and noun or infinitive}} to be in or [[fall into]] (a condition, state, or action, generally a bad or undesirable one) {{defdate|since the Middle Kingdom}}
#: {{coi|egy|{{egy-h|wA-A-N31-r-wAs-nDs}}|tr='''wꜣj''' r wꜣs|t='''to be''' in disrepair, '''to be''' in ruins}}
# {{senseid|egy|come}} to be(come) [[present]], to [[come]], to [[arrive]] (+ {{m|egy|r}}: to, in; + {{m|egy|m}}: from) {{defdate|Middle Kingdom and (chiefly) Greco-Roman Period}}
#: {{coi|egy|{{egy-h|pH-r:*r-D54-r-s-W-H-TAw-m-wA-i-i-D54:f}}{{ref|{{R:egy:DZA|22.044.580}}}}|tr=pḥrr r swḥ m '''wꜣy'''.f|running faster than the wind at its '''coming'''}}
# {{lb|egy|with {{m|egy|m}}}} to get [[involved]] with (something bad) {{defdate|Old Kingdom}}

=====Usage notes=====
The exact meaning and nuances of {{senseno|egy|fall into|pos=false}} ‘fall into (a condition etc.)’ are disputed:
* The traditional view, detailed by {{w|James Henry Breasted|Breasted}} in 1901, is that the sense is [[inceptive]]/[[inchoative]], thus ‘to begin to’, ‘to be about to’, or the like, and that it is a separate verb from ‘to be far from’.{{ref|{{w|James Henry Breasted|Breasted, James Henry}} (1901), “[https://archive.org/details/proceedings23soci/page/236 Varia]” in ''Proceedings of the Society of Biblical Archaeology'', volume 23, pages 236–243}} However, this interpretation fits poorly in many cases.
* {{w|Joachim Friedrich Quack|Quack}} instead proposes that this is a euphemistic use of the sense ‘to be far from’ to mean essentially its opposite, in order to avoid a taboo of mentioning bad happenings in association with the king, gods, temples, Egypt, and the like. (Such euphemistic reversals are attested elsewhere in Egyptian texts.) {{senseno|egy|come|cap=1|pos=false}} ‘to come’ would also stem from such a euphemistic usage of ‘be far from’.{{ref|name=Quack}}
* Depuydt proposes that the sense of the verb is simply ‘to be far’ in all instances, but that the following preposition {{m|egy|r}} should be interpreted as ‘toward’ instead of ‘from’ in {{senseno|egy|fall into|pos=false}}: thus ‘to be (or proceed) far toward’, ‘to be just about’, ‘to be in an advanced stage of’.{{ref|Depuydt, Leo (1998) “‘Far Toward’: A Common Hieroglyphic Idiom” in [https://ihac.nenu.edu.cn/ENGLISH/JAC/former_volumes.htm ''Journal of Ancient Civilizations'', volume 13], pages 39–46}} Franke presents a similar interpretation.{{ref|Franke, Detlef (1998) “Das Entfernen eines Sprachtabus: Nochmals zur Konstruktion {{m|egy||wꜣj r}}” in ''Göttinger Miszellen'', volume 165, pages 51–56}}{{att|egy|I do not have access right now to Franke’s article, only Depuydt’s description of it. Needs to be checked when access is available, or by someone who does have access.}}

