nj:
===Etymology 1===
From {{suffix|egy|n|gloss1=to, for|j|pos2=nisba ending}}.

====Pronunciation====
{{egy-pr|o=nij|m=nij|ml=nə|l=nə}}

====Adjective====
{{egy-adj|head=n:y}}

# [[of]], [[belonging]] to
# [[make|made]] of (a [[material]] or [[composition]])
# [[of]], [[possess]]ing (a [[quality]])
# {{ng|introduces the agent of an infinitive when it is a personal pronoun}}

=====Usage notes=====
This ''genitival adjective'' can be used to express the indirect genitive. In this case, it indicates that the noun preceding it, with which it agrees in gender and number, is possessed by the noun which follows it.

In Late Egyptian the functional contrasts of characterization versus specification that distinguished the indirect and direct genitive disappeared, and outside a restricted set of particular words the indirect genitive with {{m|egy||nj}} largely supplanted the direct genitive without it.

=====Inflection=====
{{egy-decl-adj|f=n|nj, n|hiero1={{egy-h|n:y}}, {{egy-h|n}}|hiero2=n:t
|hiero3=n-w&y|hiero4=n:t*y
|hiero5={{egy-h|nw}}, {{egy-h|nw:Z1}}|hiero6=n:t}}

In Late Egyptian {{m|egy||nj}} is usually not inflected by gender and number but invariably appears as {{m|egy||n}}. Sometimes the writings of the former feminine and plural forms are used interchangeably with the masculine singular without distinction. The exception is in more formal texts, where the old distinctions and inflections are sometimes still used.

=====Alternative forms=====
{{egy-hieroforms
