|nt-ḥsb
|nt-ḥtrj
|nḏt|sn-n-mwt}}

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|cop|ⲛ̄-|ⲙ̄-}}

===Etymology 2===
Oréal hypothesizes that {{m|egy||nj}} originated as a verb expressing nonexistence or absence in a stative construction, drawing a speculative connection with the attested verb {{m|egy|nj||to rebuff, to drive away}}.<ref>Oréal, Elsa (2022) “The negative existential cycle in Ancient Egyptian” in Ljuba Veselinova & Arja Hamari (eds.), ''The Negative Existential Cycle'', Berlin: Language Science Press, pages 197–230</ref>

A cognate relation to {{cog|sem-pro|*lā||no, not}} has also been suggested.

====Particle====
{{egy-part|proclitic|head=D35}}

# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian|with a following noun or pronoun}} [[there is]] [[no]], [[there are]] no; {{ng|introduces an independent negated existential clause}}
# {{lb|egy|Old Egyptian}} [[not]]; {{ng|negates any sentence}}
# {{lb|egy|Middle Egyptian}} [[not]]; {{ng|negates a nominal sentence, an adjectival sentence of possession, or the [[rheme]] of an emphatic clause}} (used with {{m|egy|js}}; see Usage Notes below)
# {{lb|egy|Middle Egyptian}} [[not]]; {{ng|negates most verbal predicates besides infinitival, imperative, and subjunctive forms; forms the negation of the perfect, perfect passive, terminative, perfective, imperfective, prospective, and prospective passive.}}
# [[no]], [[not]] [[any]]; {{ng|negates an individual noun}}
# [[not]]; {{ng|negates other individual words}}

=====Usage notes=====
* When used alone, {{m|egy||nj}} negates the individual word or verbal predicate following it.

* When negating (nonverbal) nominal sentences, adjectival sentences of possession (which start with the genitival adjective {{m|egy|nj|n(j)}} {{egy-h|n}}), and rhemes of emphatic clauses, this particle is typically followed by the first element of the negated clause and then the negative particle {{m|egy|js}}. In Middle Egyptian it cannot negate adjectival sentences which do not indicate possession, nor adverbial sentences, which are instead negated by {{m|egy|nn}}. It also cannot negate verbal sentences that are not emphatic, so the presence of a verb between {{m|egy||nj}} and {{m|egy|js}} always indicates an emphatic clause.

* {{m|egy||nj}} combines with a number of other words to form negative particles with more specialised meanings, for which see the next section.

