This demonstrative is a pronoun, and so does not directly modify nouns. In Middle Egyptian it becomes used as a demonstrative for plural nouns in place of the old adjectives {{m|egy|jpw}} and {{m|egy|jptw}} and comes to serve as a plural [[definite article]]. When used in this way, it precedes the noun, with the genitival adjective {{m|egy|nj|n(j)}} in between, e.g. "these feet" is {{m|egy||nw n(j) rdw}} (literally "this of feet").

In Old Egyptian it forms a contrastive pair with the demonstrative pronoun {{m|egy|nf}}, in which {{m|egy||nw}} is [[proximal]].

