<div>There is some disagreement about how to read this word and its ideogram {{egy-h|niwt}}; the German tradition (e.g. the ''Wörterbuch der ägyptischen Sprache'') tends to read {{m|egy|nt}} or {{m|egy||nwt}}, while Loprieno reads {{m|egy|nt|nʔt}} with an unwritten but phonemic glottal stop, and the Anglo-American tradition (e.g. Gardiner, Allen, Faulkner) reads {{m|egy|njwt}}.</div>

{{box|title=Evidence for each reading|
Gardiner supports the reading {{m|egy|njwt}} with reference to the ideogram’s use in {{egy-h|n:t-niwt-N50-tyw}} {{m|egy|nwtjw|n(jw)tjw{{sup|?}}|those belonging to the lower sky}}, a word derived from {{m|egy|njwt|nj(w)t|lower sky}} attested in the Pyramid Texts. The German readings {{m|egy|nt}} or {{m|egy||nwt}} are supported by a writing of {{egy-h|T:f-niwt}} for the name of the [[goddess]] [[Tefnut]] ({{m|egy|tfnwt}} or {{m|egy|tfnt}}) in the {{w|Amduat}} IV 48, as well as by the use of the word in place of {{m|egy||nt}} in writings of {{m|egy|ḥnt||pelican}}. The reading of {{m|egy||nwt}} may be supported by {{w|Diodorus Siculus}}, who claims that Thebes {{egy-h|niwt:t*Z1}} was named after Osiris’s mother, presumably the goddess [[Nut]] ({{m|egy||nwt}}). Loprieno’s reading with a glottal stop is supported by the Hebrew rendering {{m|he|נֹא|tr=nōʾ}} of the name of Thebes, as well as its Akkadian transcription {{m|akk|𒉌𒀪|tr=né-eʾ, ni-iʾ}}. Ambiguous evidence includes the very late variant writing {{egy-h|niwt-kA:r-T:niwt}} {{m|egy|nwtkꜣrṯ|n(j)wtkꜣrṯ}} for Ancient Greek {{m|grc|Ναύκρατις}} and the (late) Greek rendering of the word as {{m|grc||-νη-}} in the name of the pharaoh {{w|Psusennes I|Psusennes}}.
}}

