=====Alternative forms=====
The first person singular suffix pronoun is often not written; particularly, it is regularly omitted in Old Egyptian. When it is written, it has a number of variants:

{{egy-hieroforms
|i|read1=.j|date1=Old Kingdom
|i-A1|read2=.j
|i-Z1|read3=.j|date3=Middle Kingdom
|Hn|read4=.j|date4=18th Dynasty|note4=sportive writing, from<br>{{m|egy|j||reed panicle}}
|Z1|read5=.j|date5=Late Period}}

Further, it can optionally be varied to indicate the identity of the antecedent — a distinction which would not have been indicated in speech, e.g.:

{{egy-hieroforms
|A1|note1=male human
|B1|note2=female human
|A40|note3=god
|G7|note4=god or king
|A41|note5=king
|A42|note6=king
|A50|note7=dead human
|t|date8=Late Egyptian|note8=female human}}

<div>In Late Egyptian, the presence of this pronoun results in the retention of final {{m|egy||-t}} when the word it is attached to ends in this consonant; this is sometimes expressed by leaving the pronoun unwritten but adding {{egy-h|t-W}} to indicate the retained {{m|egy||-t}}.</div>

