𓄿:
[[File:Hieroglyphs from the tomb of Seti I.jpg|right|thumb|Colorful hieroglyphs from the tomb of Seti I, showing 𓄿.]]

===Glyph origin===
Representing the [[Egyptian vulture]] ({{taxfmt|Neophron percnopterus|species}}). The sharp “corner” at the back of the head represents a tuft of feathers sticking out; other details sometimes shown include a featherless area at the base of the beak and a lean, thinly feathered neck. {{egy-hiero-color|polychrome|2=y}} The oldest depictions color the wings blue or grey, but later they become green; the glyph has a largely yellow head, and yellow or red feet. (Less careful representations are sometimes all yellow, all blue, or nearly all white.) The phonetic value of {{m|egy||ꜣ}} is derived by the rebus principle from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|ꜣ||Egyptian vulture, bird}}. Compare the Chinese character {{m|zh|鳥|sc=Hani}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|A}}|tr=ꜣ}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||ꜣ}}.}}
# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|ꜣ||Egyptian vulture, bird}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This glyph should not be confused with the extremely similar {{egy-h|tyw}} ({{m|egy|𓅂}}, a buzzard), which serves as a phonogram for {{m|egy||tjw}} and has a more rounded back of the head. In some inscriptions the two could also be distinguished by their colors, the buzzard being largely yellow with red details.</div>

