𓈖:
{{wp}}

===Glyph origin===
A depiction of waves. The number of ripples varies, but the ends always slant down and can sometimes be longer than the other strokes. In less careful writing, the hieroglyph can be found simplified to a single horizontal line, sometimes rising at the end. Rarely, it can be turned on its side to stand vertically. {{egy-hiero-color|black|nodot=y}}<!-- or grey? [source?]-->, or dark blue,[[Category:Blue Egyptian hieroglyphs]] suggestive of silt-laden Nile flood water which watered the "black land" ({{m|egy|kmt}}). (Compare the canal glyph {{m|egy|𓈘}}, where the water is most often green, and the pool glyph {{m|egy|𓈙}}, where the water was lighter blue or green.) Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|巛|sc=Hani}}. 

The phonogrammatic value is possibly derived by the [[rebus principle]] from {{m|egy|nt||water}}, but this word only appears in the Middle Kingdom. An alternative source could be the older {{m|egy|nwyt||swell of water}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|n}}|tr=n}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||n}}, as for example in the preposition {{m|egy|n||to, for}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] in {{m|egy|ḏt||serf}}, by confusion with {{egy-h|N17}} ({{m|egy|𓇿}}).}}
# {{ng|Used in {{egy-h|mw}} ({{m|egy|𓈗}}).}}

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|490}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|11}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

𓊃:
===Glyph origin===
Representing a door bolt used in double doors. The bulges in the center kept the bolt restrained between two fastening rings on one door; slid from one end to the other, it would pass through a third fastening ring on the other door and so lock it. When summarized, the bulges could be reduced to a pair of rounded dots. {{egy-hiero-color|red}} The phonogrammatic value derives by the [[rebus principle]] from its use as a logogram for {{m|egy|z||door bolt}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|z}}|tr=z, s}}

# {{ng|[[uniliteral|Uniliteral]] [[phonogram]] for {{m|egy||z}}; after Old Egyptian, this phoneme merged with {{m|egy||s}}, so this glyph became an alternative phonogram for {{m|egy||s}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|z||door bolt; being; type of fish}}.}}
# {{ng|[[logogram|Logogram]] for {{m|egy|ḫm||Letopolis}}, by confusion with {{egy-h|R22}} ({{m|egy|𓋉}}).}} {{defdate|since the Middle Kingdom}}

====Usage notes====
Homophonic to {{egy-h|s}} ({{m|egy|𓋴}}) after Old Egyptian, when the two sounds merged.

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|p=496}}
* {{R:egy:Calligraphy|12}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* {{R:egy:Peust|48}}

𓋴:
===Glyph origin===
