𓊌:
===Glyph origin===
<div>Representing a cut block of stone or brick. The dimension of the sign varies, and it may be written long like the pool glyph {{m|egy|𓈙}} ({{egy-h|S}}), with which it should not be confused; the stone glyph was often written shorter to distinguish it. The stone glyph was conventionally colored either white<!-- or yellow--> (in many examples, representing a whitish stone such as limestone) or blue (in other examples, perhaps representing a mud-brick—as Egyptian scribes generally used a limited color palette that did not include grey or brown—or a block of dark stone such as basalt or greywacke, although blue coloration has also been interpreted as mere confusion with the pool glyph).<!--ref: Nunn, below--></div>

Compare the Chinese character {{m|mul|石|sc=Hani}}.

===Symbol===
{{head|egy|symbol|head={{egy-h|O39}}}}

# {{ng|Logogram for {{m|egy|jnr||stone}}.}}
# {{ng|[[determinative|Determinative]] for stone, as in {{m|egy|jnr||stone}}, {{m|egy|ꜥꜣt||precious stone}}, {{m|egy|dbn||deben, a measure of weight}}, {{m|egy|ꜥr||pebble}}, {{m|egy|ḏbt||brick}}.}}

====Usage notes====
<div>This glyph and {{egy-h|S}} ({{m|egy|𓈙}}) are extremely similar; see the usage notes there regarding typical coloration and other distinguishing features of the two.</div>

===References===
* {{R:egy:Gardiner|497}}
* {{R:egy:Betrò}}
* David Nunn, [https://www.phrp.be/Palaeography.php A Palaeography of Polychrome Hieroglyphs] (2020)

